    <text>
        <front>
            <div type="summary">
                <p>
                    Letter from Dartmouth College's English professor Benfield Pressey requesting permission to reprint "How Should One Read a Book?" not from Hogarth's Common Reader Second Series but rather from a reprint in the Yale Review: 'For our purposes this earlier form is more desirable than the later form.'
                </p>
            </div>
        </front>


        <body>
            <div type="letter">
                <opener>
                    <add place="top-left">
                        MS 2750/553/68
                    </add>
                    <dateline>
                        <placeName key="Q131908">
                            {Hanover, N. H.}
                        </placeName>
                        <date when="1947-12-29">
                            29 December 1947
                        </date>
                    </dateline>


                    <address>
                        <addrLine>
                            <orgName key="Q1623362">
                                Hogarth Press
                            </orgName>
                        </addrLine>
                        <addrLine>
                            40-42 <placeName key="Q100315459">William IV Street</placeName>
                        </addrLine>
                        <addrLine>
                            <placeName key="Q84">London </placeName> W.C.2
                        </addrLine>
                        <addrLine>
                            <placeName key="Q21">
                                England
                            </placeName>
                        </addrLine>
                    </address>


                    <stamp>
                        Hogarth press stamp
                        <mentioned>RECEIVED</mentioned>
                        <mentioned><date when="1948-01-02">2 JAN 1948</date></mentioned>
                    </stamp>


                    <salute>
                        Gentlemen,
                    </salute>
                </opener>


                <p>
                    You have been so kind as to give my collaborator, <persName key="Q59629401">Professor 
                    <lb/>Reginald E. Watters</persName> of the <orgName key="Q391028">University of British Columbia</orgName>, per
                    <lb/>mission to reprint in an anthology of essays intended for college 
                    <lb/>English classes <persName key="Q40909">Virginia Woolf</persName>'s <title key="Q140677081">How Should One Read a Book?</title><note><p>This essay was twice published by Virginia Woolf. The first time was on 1 September 1926 in the 'Yale Review', while the second time was in 1932 as part of the essay collection 'The Second Common Reader'.</p></note><supplied>.</supplied>
                    <lb/><persName>Mr. Watters</persName> has also obtained permission from <orgName key="Q83521071">Harcourt, Brace &amp; 
                    <lb/>Company</orgName>, American publishers of <title><emph rend="underlined">The Second Common Reader</emph></title> <note><p>'The Second Common Reader' (also published as 'The Common Reader: Second Series') is a collection of Virginia Woolf's essays published in 1932 by The Hogarth Press.</p></note>, in 
                    <lb/>which an essay with this title appears. As you no doubt know, 
                    <lb/>however, this essay had an earlier form, which appeared in the 
                    <lb/><title key="Q2599084"><emph rend="underlined">Yale Review</emph></title> in <date when="1926">1926</date> and was reprinted in <title><emph rend="underlined">The Yale Review Anth
                    <lb/>ology</emph></title> in <date when="1942">1942</date>. For our purposes this earlier form is more 
                    <lb/>desirable than the later form, which, as you remember, came at 
                    <lb/>the end of <title><emph rend="underlined">The Second Common Reader</emph></title> and assumed acquaintance 
                    <lb/>with what had been said in the preceding essays of that book. 
                    <lb/>The <date when="1926">1926</date> form of <persName>Mrs. Woolf</persName>'s essay has the greater independence 
                    <lb/>and is therefore more teachable in such an anthology as ours.
                </p>


                <p>
                    May we have your permission therefore to reprint the essay 
                    <lb/>as it stood in the <title><emph rend="underlined">Yale Review,</emph></title> rather than in <title><emph rend="underlined">The Second Common 
                    <lb/>Reader</emph></title>?<supplied>.</supplied> I am venturing to address you instead of <persName>Mr. Watters</persName> as 
                    <lb/>I am nearer both to you and to our publishers, who are <orgName key="Q845617">Scribner's</orgName>. 
                    <lb/>In order that I may have prompt reply I enclose 18 cents in 
                    <lb/>international reply coupons <note><p>International reply coupons enabled a sender to pay for the recipient's reply postage in advance. They were introduced by the Universal Postal Union (UPU) in 1906 and discontinued in 2026.</p></note>, and I beg that you will use them to 
                    <lb/>reply by air mail. If you grant this permission, we will of course 
                    <lb/>make acknowledgement as you direct, either with or without mention 
                    <lb/>of <title><emph rend="underlined">The Second Common Reader</emph></title>. The payment we asked was four guineas, 
                    <lb/>and <orgName>Harcourt, Brace</orgName> asked &#36;40. We wish to obtain distribution rights 
                    <lb/>for both the United States and Canada.
                </p>


                <closer>
                    <salute>
                        Yours sincerely,
                    </salute>
                    <signed>
                        <add place="above">
                            <persName>Benfield Pressey</persName>
                        </add>
                        <persName>Benfield Pressey</persName>
                        <lb/><roleName>Professor of English</roleName>
                    </signed>
                    <add place="right"><emph rend="underlined">Agree</emph></add>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
